 @Cujobob  I see where you're coming from, I think about switching to Nvidia again because AMD is just not triple screen friendly and has some issues when it comes to the fps when calculating the correct perspective in sim racers (you need around 4-5 times the amount of pixels for proper triple screen use). Nividia cards don't struggle with this. Next, you just can't compensate the bezels, if your game doesn't support an ingame bezel compensation you are stuck with a crooked experience. 
Besides that, AMD draws less power under load and even when propelling 3 screens in windows, which I need to do jobwise, it draws around half of the watts of newer Nivida cards. Not an easy decission, I might stick with it just to save money on electricity as my PC is running 24/7 for like 8-10 hours daily. Raytracing is not really a thing for me yet, I'm okay with the developer's workarounds so far, I think, it's absolutely an upcoming standard, but still at its very beginning. In 2 years, RT-games will also struggle with RTX 3000s, would be interesting to see if AMD can catch up.


edit: Yesterday I found the hidden program with which you can compensate the bezels, finally! It's not good that you need to search for it, should be a legit option right from the beginning. Thus, one argument for Nvidia is gone, good for AMD.

